WEBVTT

00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,932
This is a new, live Survive Drive session.

00:00:02,932 --> 00:00:07,329
I am setting a Santa Clarita start and a Long Beach destination.

00:00:07,329 --> 00:00:13,925
The map and route candidates are still empty, so you can follow the search from its first click.

00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,079
Before searching, I review the Valhalla road controls.

00:01:30,079 --> 00:01:34,697
Highway and toll preferences stay at their neutral defaults for this example.

00:01:34,697 --> 00:01:41,625
We can change them here, but the new search would then need its own route and evidence review.

00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:03,245
I request four suggested escape gates near each end of the trip.

00:03:03,245 --> 00:03:07,200
The circles and road suggestions appear in the app itself.

00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:15,900
With the no-gate choice on both ends, four gates each way create five times five, or twenty five, start and destination pairings.

00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,029
The local Valhalla server now searches those twenty five pairings.

00:04:31,029 --> 00:04:33,850
It returns twenty seven distinct route candidates.

00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:40,700
This is real route data for this session; no property lookup was needed to make the routes.

00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:43,560
Round Zero ranks the whole candidate pool using evidence already saved in the shared cache.

00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:51,640
Each of these twenty seven candidates has less than twenty percent known coverage, so the first screen protects them all.

00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:55,276
An included route is eligible for another evidence round.

00:05:55,276 --> 00:05:57,700
It is not a driving recommendation.

00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:27,373
Five new lookups per route would exceed the current batch limit, so I set the quota to three.

00:07:27,373 --> 00:07:31,975
I also briefly deselect and reselect a candidate to show the estimate respond.

00:07:31,975 --> 00:07:35,870
The free cache check then finds twenty six unique, uncached cells.

00:07:35,870 --> 00:07:41,889
At one credit and one cent per new lookup, this round is capped at twenty six cents.

00:07:41,889 --> 00:07:45,075
I start it only after seeing that exact count.

00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:55,001
The first RentCast round is underway.

00:08:55,001 --> 00:08:58,753
Each returned cell updates every route that shares it.

00:08:58,753 --> 00:09:04,171
The twenty six calls succeed, and the app reranks all twenty seven candidates.

00:09:04,171 --> 00:09:07,506
The next included pool narrows to eleven routes.

00:09:07,506 --> 00:09:12,925
Unknown cells remain unknown; the app does not treat them as low-value cells.

00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,004
Here I compare the new route cards.

00:10:48,004 --> 00:10:53,582
Distance, travel time, known coverage, and weighted price per square foot all matter.

00:10:53,582 --> 00:10:57,443
The next free check identifies fourteen new shared-cell lookups.

00:10:57,443 --> 00:11:01,305
After those fourteen calls finish, seven routes remain included.

00:11:01,305 --> 00:11:06,025
An excluded route can reenter if later evidence changes the ranking.

00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:49,471
The map shows the selected route, its colored property cells, and the highway segments excluded from the property comparison.

00:12:49,471 --> 00:12:53,404
I also inspect a cell away from the candidate route.

00:12:53,404 --> 00:13:00,875
That single extra lookup is clearly identified as outside the route, so it does not change the route rankings.

00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,497
The third evidence plan calls for ten new cells.

00:15:03,497 --> 00:15:07,772
I review the separate Run control, then let the requests finish.

00:15:07,772 --> 00:15:10,880
The included pool falls from seven to four.

00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:17,875
All four rounds keep the culling percentage at thirty five percent, with whole-route rounding shown in the interface.

00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:55,549
One more exact plan uses nine new lookups.

00:16:55,549 --> 00:17:01,266
When it completes, two routes are included and twenty five are excluded from further sampling.

00:17:01,266 --> 00:17:07,745
The shorter finalist is sixty six point three four miles and seventy five and a half minutes.

00:17:07,745 --> 00:17:12,700
It has about eighty one percent known cell coverage and six unknown cells.

00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:57,375
I move into Look around and select a known route cell for inspection.

00:18:57,375 --> 00:19:05,250
Street View can add visual context, but its imagery date matters, and the property value signal is not a safety verdict.

00:19:05,250 --> 00:19:12,375
The recording ends at this review point; a route choice should follow your own inspection and current road conditions.
